1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
Beware.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:05,500
You may have just seconds to live.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,000
Some of the world's most dangerous encounters

4
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,640
are closer than you think.

5
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:13,840
Danger lurks in a long, slumbering volcano,

6
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:18,760
near a major American city, and in the vortex of a massive funnel

7
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:21,760
cloud at your doorstep.

8
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:25,600
It has highly toxic fangs and can even paralyze you

9
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:27,900
with one nasty handkerchief.

10
00:00:27,901 --> 00:00:32,200
with one nasty helmet-to-helmet hit.

11
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:36,000
And don't forget to pull your chute.

12
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:39,640
Now, most dangerous on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:56,200
The climactic eruption of the Philippines' Mount Pinatubo

14
00:00:56,201 --> 00:01:00,800
on June 15, 1991, is a jolting reminder

15
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:03,540
that the world is riddled with dangerous volcanoes.

16
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:11,140
The blast propelled volcanic ash 22 miles high.

17
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:15,860
It killed more than 700 people, many when wet ash collapsed

18
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:17,660
roofs.

19
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:21,600
Massive mudflows took still more lives.

20
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:24,800
It also triggered memories of far more devastating eruptions

21
00:01:24,801 --> 00:01:32,400
eruptions in the past, like Tambora in 1815, Krakatoa in

22
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:38,640
1883, and Mount Pelé in 1902.

23
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:41,520
But Pinatubo is far from the most dangerous volcano in the

24
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:44,260
world today.

25
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:49,500
Many volcanologists say it's Italy's Mount Vesuvius.

26
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:53,720
Its last devastating eruption was in AD 79, which obliterated

27
00:01:53,721 --> 00:01:57,720
of Pompeii and Herculaneum.

28
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:00,120
Today, Naples and its surrounding communities,

29
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:06,260
population 3 million, sit on Vesuvius' front porch.

30
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:11,280
Geologic evidence suggests it's primed for a repeat.

31
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:16,080
But what is the most dangerous volcano in the United States?

32
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:18,820
You might think it's the most active.

33
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:22,020
That's Kilauea on Hawaii's Big Island.

34
00:02:22,021 --> 00:02:26,520
It's been erupting continuously for more than two decades.

35
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:29,260
But its large lava flows, which have sporadically threatened

36
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:33,560
small communities, move slowly, leaving plenty of time

37
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:36,060
to evacuate.

38
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:38,260
So Kilauea is not the most dangerous.

39
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:43,680
Scientists agree the most dangerous US volcano

40
00:02:43,780 --> 00:02:45,380
is within the North American edge

41
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:46,980
of the so-called Ring of Fire.

42
00:02:46,981 --> 00:02:47,880
of fire.

43
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:52,980
Stretching from Northern California

44
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:56,760
to Northern Washington, 13 volcanoes

45
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:59,520
are considered highly dangerous.

46
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:01,820
One of them unleashed a blast forever burned

47
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:04,700
in the American consciousness.

48
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:07,540
In March of 1980, Mount St. Helens collapsed

49
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:10,440
in the largest landslide in recorded human history.

50
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:13,440
Huge wall of rock came rushing down off the volcano,

51
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:15,980
slammed into Johnson Bridge, bounced off it,

52
00:03:15,981 --> 00:03:19,020
and traveled 14 miles down the Tudor River Valley,

53
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:21,420
filling it to an average depth of 150 feet.

54
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,180
And if we had been standing right here watching this explosion,

55
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:26,520
we would have had 43 seconds to live.

56
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:28,020
Get off the bridge!

57
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:28,920
Get off!

58
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:29,820
He's doing it.

59
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:36,440
The eruption killed 57 people

60
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:39,040
and devastated hundreds of square miles of forest land.

61
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:44,840
Mount St. Helens is still an active volcano,

62
00:03:44,841 --> 00:03:47,640
But it's not the most dangerous.

63
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:51,180
America's most dangerous volcano is a mere 50 miles

64
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:53,080
to the north.

65
00:03:53,180 --> 00:03:59,380
It's Mount Rainier, a 14,400-foot peak looming over Seattle

66
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:02,800
and Tacoma, the most densely populated region

67
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:06,000
in the Pacific Northwest.

68
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:10,240
The volcano hasn't erupted in over 150 years.

69
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:12,440
But its frequent tremors serve notice

70
00:04:12,441 --> 00:04:16,140
that it's geologically alive and kicking.

71
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:17,580
It's an active volcano.

72
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:19,380
That's the first thing I want to stress.

73
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:21,240
Many people believe it isn't.

74
00:04:21,340 --> 00:04:23,920
But it is indeed a very active volcano.

75
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:26,460
Surprisingly, it's not an eruption of searing lava

76
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:30,360
and ash that scientists fear most,

77
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:35,560
but a terrifying phenomenon that an eruption could trigger.

78
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:38,740
In the 1950s, geologist Rocky Crandall

79
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:41,900
found the evidence in fossilized mud deposits,

80
00:04:41,901 --> 00:04:46,200
along Raniere's watershed valleys.

81
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:49,400
It's called Lahar, an Indonesian word

82
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:52,340
meaning volcanic mudflow.

83
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:54,840
This was the devastating phenomenon that occurred when

84
00:04:54,940 --> 00:04:58,660
Mount St. Helens erupted in 1980.

85
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:03,760
And it last happened on Raniere just 500 years ago.

86
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:07,300
Raniere's past Lahar swiftly buried many square miles

87
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:10,160
in up to 30 feet of mud.

88
00:05:10,161 --> 00:05:14,400
This same region today is home to approximately 100,000 people

89
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:18,400
in communities neighboring Seattle and Tacoma.

90
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,800
The potential for another mass of lahar

91
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:24,300
is lurking in Rainier's snowy cap.

92
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:27,120
The volcano contains approximately one cubic mile

93
00:05:27,220 --> 00:05:31,080
of glacial ice, as much as exists on the other 12

94
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:32,720
Cascades volcanoes combined.

95
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:38,860
An earthquake, heavy rainfall, or even gravity's pull

96
00:05:38,861 --> 00:05:41,900
could trigger a lahar.

97
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,400
But most begin when hot rocks from an eruption

98
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:47,460
melt snow and ice.

99
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:49,680
The milk water picks up loose rock

100
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:52,440
and creates a slurry of mud and boulders,

101
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:56,880
reaching speeds of up to 70 miles per hour.

102
00:05:56,980 --> 00:05:59,340
It could be just an unheralded collapse

103
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:01,220
where the mountain just gives way.

104
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:05,380
Weak mountain, lots of soil, starts washing down the Puyallup

105
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:08,320
River Valley, picks up more and more trees,

106
00:06:08,321 --> 00:06:10,960
It gets bigger and bigger as it goes, lots of liquid.

107
00:06:11,060 --> 00:06:14,220
It's probably moving 60 or 70 miles per hour

108
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:15,420
in the upper valley here.

109
00:06:19,260 --> 00:06:21,460
Just 30 miles from Mount Rainier,

110
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:24,800
the most endangered are the 5,500 residents

111
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:27,580
of the city of Hording.

112
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,340
They would have less than 40 minutes

113
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:30,780
before the deluge is upon them.

114
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:36,480
And it takes them close to that amount of time

115
00:06:36,481 --> 00:06:38,280
out of their school and out of the valley.

116
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:39,780
I think it wouldn't be difficult, actually,

117
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:41,180
to get everyone out of there.

118
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:44,080
I mean, certainly the hope is that we could.

119
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:46,620
Fleeing motorists would clog the few exit roads

120
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:49,720
out of the valley, causing gridlock.

121
00:06:49,820 --> 00:06:53,640
Lahar drills teach residents to run immediately for high ground

122
00:06:53,740 --> 00:06:55,640
to escape the unrelenting flow.

123
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:03,540
The brutal scenario's long-term impacts are sobering.

124
00:07:03,541 --> 00:07:07,140
A Mount Rainier event would not just be a Pierce County event.

125
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:11,580
It would be truly a regional event, a statewide event.

126
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,680
Potentially thousands dead.

127
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:18,980
Major shipping ports in Tacoma and Seattle paralyzed.

128
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:23,900
Transportation routes and airports shut down.

129
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,240
A large lahar could dwarf the human and economic toll

130
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:29,240
of Hurricane Katrina.

131
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:32,300
So we're not just looking at a temporary displacement.

132
00:07:32,301 --> 00:07:36,640
displacement, it could be a complete relocation effort.

133
00:07:36,641 --> 00:07:42,600
And economically, it's going to have a huge impact.

134
00:07:42,601 --> 00:07:45,160
Some Washington residents doubt this grim scenario

135
00:07:45,161 --> 00:07:47,200
will ever happen.

136
00:07:47,201 --> 00:07:50,140
But their reality check lies in the tragedy triggered by

137
00:07:50,141 --> 00:07:55,500
Colombia's Nevado del Ruiz volcano in 1985, which, like

138
00:07:55,501 --> 00:07:58,340
Rainier, has a large ice cap.

139
00:07:58,341 --> 00:08:02,160
Several hours after a small eruption, a lahar charged down

140
00:08:02,161 --> 00:08:05,260
slammed into the city of Armero with several feet of mud

141
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:06,660
similar to wet concrete.

142
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:13,300
More than 23,000 people perished.

143
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:18,980
And it's estimated that if those people in Armero had had

144
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:22,140
any type of early warning, they could have comfortably

145
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:23,280
walked to safety.

146
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:25,720
It was a matter of getting to high ground.

147
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:29,480
That was our learning moment.

148
00:08:29,481 --> 00:08:33,520
In the late 1990s, US Geological Survey scientists

149
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:36,120
built a lahar early warning and detection system

150
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:37,020
from Mount Rainier.

151
00:08:40,020 --> 00:08:42,920
The system depends on 10 remotely located acoustic

152
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:48,540
flow monitors, or AFMs, basically electronic ears

153
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:51,840
listening for signs of a lahar.

154
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:54,540
This AFM site sits up higher here.

155
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:56,840
It's designed to stay intact for a mud flow.

156
00:08:56,940 --> 00:08:58,280
It's got a radio inside of it.

157
00:08:58,281 --> 00:08:59,620
Every nine minutes, it transmits,

158
00:08:59,720 --> 00:09:03,620
and we receive that at a computer down at our base station.

159
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:06,620
It's got the antenna there and the solar panels,

160
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:10,120
batteries inside of it, so it's totally self-contained.

161
00:09:10,220 --> 00:09:12,360
When it detects a lahar, the station

162
00:09:12,460 --> 00:09:15,040
will increase transmissions to every three minutes

163
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:20,040
to emergency responders in 911 centers.

164
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:23,480
The chances that an AFM will cry wolf are nil.

165
00:09:23,580 --> 00:09:25,080
The challenge was a lot of lahars

166
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:27,640
were generated during eruptions of volcanoes.

167
00:09:27,641 --> 00:09:32,480
So there's earthquakes, there's explosions, tremor.

168
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:35,580
So we had to have a very focused sensor,

169
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:37,280
one that was sensitive to lahars,

170
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:40,680
but not to all the other goings on.

171
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:43,180
Each AFM owes its keen sensitivity

172
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:48,200
to an acoustic geophone buried five feet below the ground.

173
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:51,760
The geophone is fine-tuned to ignore low-amplitude signatures

174
00:09:51,860 --> 00:09:54,100
from tremors.

175
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:57,060
It only responds to the distinct high-amplitude signature

176
00:09:57,061 --> 00:10:00,960
of a lahar and triggers the warning signal.

177
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:05,900
Three of the 10 AFM stations are designed as failsafe alarms.

178
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,440
They were intentionally placed low in the valley,

179
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:11,080
directly in the path of a future lahar.

180
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:13,080
What we've got here is our, this

181
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:15,540
is what we call one of our dead man sites.

182
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:19,240
This log here is designed to get washed away

183
00:10:19,340 --> 00:10:21,780
if there's a big mudflow or a lahar.

184
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:24,880
And kind of hints the term that, you know,

185
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:27,060
the site's going to go dead.

186
00:10:27,061 --> 00:10:30,900
The log is hardwired to the AFM's transmitter.

187
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,560
If the log washes away, the signal goes dead,

188
00:10:34,660 --> 00:10:37,000
and emergency responders know what's headed their way.

189
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,500
Scientists estimate that in our lifetime,

190
00:10:42,600 --> 00:10:44,020
there's about a one in seven chance

191
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:48,020
that a massive lahar sweeps this log up in a torrent that

192
00:10:48,120 --> 00:10:51,960
devastates the communities surrounding Rainier.

193
00:10:52,060 --> 00:10:54,260
But if that one chance plays out,

194
00:10:54,261 --> 00:10:57,500
the magnitude of the disaster will no doubt increase

195
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:00,500
as more communities sprout in the Lahar's projected path.

196
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:09,400
While Rainier ranks as the most dangerous volcano today,

197
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:13,820
the ultimate big blow could occur some 500 miles east,

198
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:17,520
hundreds of thousands of years from now.

199
00:11:17,620 --> 00:11:20,320
The pristine wilderness and bubbling geysers of Yellowstone

200
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:24,260
National Park offer subtle proof of what slumbers below.

201
00:11:24,261 --> 00:11:29,060
An immense 1,300-square-mile supervolcano

202
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:32,900
with a magma load estimated at 4,000 cubic miles.

203
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:39,000
Geologic evidence dating back more than 2 million years

204
00:11:39,100 --> 00:11:42,420
proves the giant has erupted at least three times

205
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:45,920
with almost incomprehensible force.

206
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:48,960
It's just on a scale that's really difficult

207
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:52,560
to get your head around, because you're talking, you know,

208
00:11:52,561 --> 00:11:57,560
you know, 1,000 times more than 1980 St. Helens,

209
00:11:57,660 --> 00:12:00,360
it's going to devastate, you know, areas that are going

210
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:04,500
to be tens to hundreds of kilometers around the volcano.

211
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:10,520
If Yellowstone delivers a supersized encore,

212
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:12,920
the final death toll could be in the millions.

213
00:12:15,420 --> 00:12:18,760
While some people play the odds, building their dream homes

214
00:12:18,860 --> 00:12:21,520
in the shadows of hell's own vents,

215
00:12:21,521 --> 00:12:25,060
Others gleefully pull on a pair of high boots and chaps

216
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:27,560
to catch America's most dangerous snake.

217
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:39,180
This is the most dangerous snake in the United States,

218
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:43,180
the Western Diamondback rattlesnake.

219
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:45,920
But that doesn't stop Jack Allen.

220
00:12:46,020 --> 00:12:48,620
He hunts the Western Diamondback in the hard-bitten lands

221
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:50,980
of West Texas.

222
00:12:50,981 --> 00:12:55,460
Its venom toxicity is moderate, but it's quick to anger

223
00:12:55,560 --> 00:12:58,760
and inhabits vast areas of the Western United States,

224
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:02,360
meaning more human encounters and more bites.

225
00:13:02,460 --> 00:13:04,500
They won't chase you or nothing.

226
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:06,900
But if you go up and try to mess with them,

227
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,040
they will bite you.

228
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:11,040
And they won't always rattle.

229
00:13:11,140 --> 00:13:13,440
They won't rattle before they bite all the time.

230
00:13:16,440 --> 00:13:19,200
As dangerous as the Western diamondback is,

231
00:13:19,201 --> 00:13:21,440
The world is full of even more lethal snakes.

232
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:26,040
Take the king cobra.

233
00:13:26,140 --> 00:13:28,640
It's the world's longest venomous snake,

234
00:13:28,740 --> 00:13:32,640
and it can deliver a huge dose of venom that's highly toxic,

235
00:13:32,740 --> 00:13:36,280
enough to kill an elephant in a single bite.

236
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:39,920
Fortunately, it rarely bites humans,

237
00:13:40,020 --> 00:13:43,220
nor does Australia's inland taipan.

238
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:46,600
But it has the most toxic venom of any snake on Earth.

239
00:13:46,601 --> 00:13:49,900
A single drop could kill you in minutes.

240
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,700
Still, it's not the most dangerous

241
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:54,940
because it's a shy, reclusive snake that humans rarely

242
00:13:55,040 --> 00:13:57,740
encounter in the wild.

243
00:13:57,840 --> 00:14:02,580
The world's most dangerous snake, the sawscale viper.

244
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:06,080
It's aggressive and inhabits heavily populated areas

245
00:14:06,180 --> 00:14:10,420
in Asia and North Africa and kills some 10,000 people

246
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:12,860
every year.

247
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:16,600
The statistics aren't nearly as shocking in the United States.

248
00:14:16,601 --> 00:14:20,240
All the same, rattlesnakes bite approximately 6,000 people

249
00:14:20,340 --> 00:14:22,540
in the United States each year.

250
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:25,140
About six die.

251
00:14:25,240 --> 00:14:27,440
The primary victims?

252
00:14:27,540 --> 00:14:31,740
Teenagers who harass or try to catch the snakes.

253
00:14:31,840 --> 00:14:35,460
A seasoned hunter like Jack is well aware of the risks.

254
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:38,960
That's why he uses the best tools for the job.

255
00:14:39,060 --> 00:14:42,260
These are a set of tongs, snake tongs we call them.

256
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:44,660
This is a hook to hook them.

257
00:14:44,661 --> 00:14:47,700
I have on extra double-lined leather boots

258
00:14:47,701 --> 00:14:49,700
that come up to my knees.

259
00:14:49,701 --> 00:14:52,200
These are a set of chaps. They're snake chaps.

260
00:14:52,201 --> 00:14:54,200
They protect my legs.

