366
00:20:44,180 --> 00:20:46,520
Venom samples are easily traced back to the donor

367
00:20:46,620 --> 00:20:49,720
with a scanner.

368
00:20:49,820 --> 00:20:51,220
So basically, what we've collected

369
00:20:51,221 --> 00:20:54,760
is about half a mil of venom.

370
00:20:54,860 --> 00:20:58,420
And this is quite a bit of venom that will do quite a bit

371
00:20:58,520 --> 00:21:03,360
of damage if a person is bitten.

372
00:21:03,460 --> 00:21:05,300
The venom is centrifuged to remove

373
00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:11,140
cellular debris and then frozen for future research.

374
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:15,680
I think what we're doing here and with other countries

375
00:21:15,780 --> 00:21:19,840
is laying groundwork for future generations to discover.

376
00:21:19,841 --> 00:21:23,440
You know, some of these species will become extinct.

377
00:21:23,540 --> 00:21:27,540
And if we don't capture their genes in bacteria,

378
00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:32,080
we will never be able to use them in drug discovery.

379
00:21:32,180 --> 00:21:34,460
Mankind has everything to gain from the venom

380
00:21:34,560 --> 00:21:37,460
of the most dangerous snakes.

381
00:21:37,560 --> 00:21:39,800
But when it comes to the most dangerous weather event

382
00:21:39,900 --> 00:21:43,200
in the United States, we have everything to lose.

383
00:21:43,300 --> 00:21:44,100
Oh, my god.

384
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:45,100
Oh, my god.

385
00:21:49,840 --> 00:21:56,840
The aftermath of a killer storm tells a brutal truth.

386
00:21:56,940 --> 00:22:01,980
In the face of nature's fury, no one is completely safe.

387
00:22:02,080 --> 00:22:04,480
What is the most dangerous weather event

388
00:22:04,580 --> 00:22:06,600
in the United States?

389
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:09,300
After the devastation wrought by Katrina,

390
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:13,700
the 2005 tragedy that took more than 1,800 lives,

391
00:22:13,800 --> 00:22:17,740
the hurricane would seem the likely answer.

392
00:22:17,741 --> 00:22:20,680
would seem the likely answer.

393
00:22:20,780 --> 00:22:23,920
But hurricanes are infrequent, easily detected,

394
00:22:24,020 --> 00:22:28,120
and often arrive with days of forewarning.

395
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:32,820
The random ferocity of lightning ranks high on the danger list.

396
00:22:32,920 --> 00:22:37,520
The air around a bolt sizzles at around 30,000 degrees,

397
00:22:37,620 --> 00:22:40,700
six times hotter than the sun's surface.

398
00:22:40,800 --> 00:22:44,660
Some 60 people a year die when struck by lightning,

399
00:22:44,661 --> 00:22:48,260
But it's not the biggest threat.

400
00:22:48,360 --> 00:22:52,100
Because of its sudden immense capacity for destruction,

401
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:55,300
the most dangerous weather event in the United States

402
00:22:55,400 --> 00:22:58,100
is the tornado.

403
00:22:58,200 --> 00:23:01,380
Tornadoes can have extremely high wind speeds,

404
00:23:01,480 --> 00:23:05,920
wind speeds as high as 250, 260 miles per hour.

405
00:23:06,020 --> 00:23:08,880
They can do tremendous damage to structures.

406
00:23:08,980 --> 00:23:10,480
They can break houses and buildings apart

407
00:23:10,580 --> 00:23:12,420
into small pieces of debris, which can hurt people

408
00:23:12,520 --> 00:23:13,520
as they fly around.

409
00:23:13,521 --> 00:23:15,720
We really aren't able to give very long leave times

410
00:23:15,721 --> 00:23:18,020
on tornadoes that tell people that they're coming.

411
00:23:18,021 --> 00:23:22,020
They usually only have minutes to respond.

412
00:23:22,021 --> 00:23:25,360
The most dangerous tornadoes, rated F2 or higher

413
00:23:25,361 --> 00:23:30,580
on the Fujita scale, appear to be random, unpredictable events.

414
00:23:30,581 --> 00:23:34,180
And they can appear in droves.

415
00:23:34,181 --> 00:23:37,080
The only predictable aspect is the geographic area

416
00:23:37,081 --> 00:23:39,820
most frequently hit.

417
00:23:39,821 --> 00:23:42,960
America's infamous tornado alley extends up

418
00:23:42,961 --> 00:23:45,800
of the United States, where warm, moist air

419
00:23:45,900 --> 00:23:48,700
from the Gulf of Mexico meets cold, dry air

420
00:23:48,800 --> 00:23:51,360
from the Rocky Mountains.

421
00:23:51,460 --> 00:23:54,940
And the presence of those two big geographic factors

422
00:23:55,040 --> 00:23:56,700
leads to us bringing the ingredients together

423
00:23:56,800 --> 00:23:59,840
for tornadoes here more than any place else on the planet.

424
00:23:59,940 --> 00:24:01,840
We got one now, and it's going down.

425
00:24:01,940 --> 00:24:04,140
It's on the ground.

426
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:07,760
Over the past half century, 311 twisters

427
00:24:07,860 --> 00:24:09,760
have taken lives in the plains.

428
00:24:09,761 --> 00:24:14,260
in the plains.

429
00:24:14,261 --> 00:24:17,600
Fewer tornadoes strike the American Southeast, states

430
00:24:17,601 --> 00:24:22,000
like Arkansas, Tennessee, Alabama, and Mississippi.

431
00:24:22,001 --> 00:24:25,500
But its residents are in even greater danger.

432
00:24:25,501 --> 00:24:26,960
The reason?

433
00:24:26,961 --> 00:24:30,120
The Southeast has the greatest concentration of mobile homes

434
00:24:30,121 --> 00:24:32,000
in the country.

435
00:24:32,001 --> 00:24:35,300
Since they lack foundations, these tornado targets

436
00:24:35,301 --> 00:24:39,060
are the most vulnerable shelters.

437
00:24:39,061 --> 00:24:42,600
mobile home residents are 15 to 20 times more likely to be

438
00:24:42,700 --> 00:24:45,360
killed in a tornado than permanent home residents are.

439
00:24:45,460 --> 00:24:48,600
That's the most dangerous place for human life in tornadoes

440
00:24:48,700 --> 00:24:50,500
in the country.

441
00:24:50,600 --> 00:24:52,900
The human toll is distressing,

442
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,720
but not when you compare it to a worst case scenario that

443
00:24:55,820 --> 00:24:57,520
someday might conceivably occur.

444
00:25:02,420 --> 00:25:04,660
Perhaps the biggest nightmare scenario

445
00:25:04,760 --> 00:25:06,460
is hitting a large sporting venue,

446
00:25:06,560 --> 00:25:08,420
say, the Texas Motor Speedway in Fort Worth

447
00:25:08,421 --> 00:25:11,720
race in early April, or the Indianapolis Motor Speedway

448
00:25:11,820 --> 00:25:13,660
during the 500 at the end of May,

449
00:25:13,760 --> 00:25:15,960
when you have hundreds of thousands of people

450
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:18,300
in a small area and in an area where,

451
00:25:18,400 --> 00:25:20,360
even under the absolute best conditions,

452
00:25:20,460 --> 00:25:23,040
it takes three to four hours to actually get everybody

453
00:25:23,140 --> 00:25:25,180
out of there.

454
00:25:25,280 --> 00:25:27,680
America's most dangerous twister to date

455
00:25:27,780 --> 00:25:31,480
occurred on March 18, 1925.

456
00:25:31,580 --> 00:25:36,740
The tri-state tornado traveled 219 miles at over 60 miles

457
00:25:36,741 --> 00:25:41,240
through Missouri, Illinois, and Indiana.

458
00:25:41,340 --> 00:25:46,380
After 3 1⁄2 hours, an estimated 695 people were dead

459
00:25:46,480 --> 00:25:49,380
and 2,000 injured.

460
00:25:49,480 --> 00:25:52,980
Two factors played a huge role in the heavy death toll,

461
00:25:53,080 --> 00:25:55,500
the lack of a tornado warning system

462
00:25:55,600 --> 00:25:57,400
and inadequate shelter.

463
00:25:57,500 --> 00:26:01,700
Two, one, fire!

464
00:26:01,800 --> 00:26:04,440
Thanks to research labs like the Wind Science Center

465
00:26:04,441 --> 00:26:09,440
Center at Texas Tech University.

466
00:26:09,540 --> 00:26:12,540
The modern storm shelter is sturdier and more resistant

467
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:17,320
to windblown debris. Scientists here built a debris

468
00:26:17,420 --> 00:26:20,680
cannon to simulate the effects of a large tornado, hurling

469
00:26:20,780 --> 00:26:25,060
building materials through the air at lethal speeds.

470
00:26:25,160 --> 00:26:30,400
In this case, a 15-pound 2x4, traveling at 100 miles per hour.

471
00:26:30,500 --> 00:26:33,840
It takes more than four layers of 3-quarter-inch plywood

472
00:26:33,841 --> 00:26:35,240
to stop this missile.

473
00:26:35,241 --> 00:26:39,240
The masonry does a good job, provided it's reinforced.

474
00:26:45,980 --> 00:26:50,160
But if it's not reinforced, then it provides little protection

475
00:26:50,161 --> 00:26:52,600
against the impact.

476
00:26:52,601 --> 00:26:56,900
So with ordinary construction, a brick veneer house

477
00:26:56,901 --> 00:26:59,300
is not safe at all.

478
00:26:59,301 --> 00:27:01,200
Studies here have led to the development

479
00:27:01,201 --> 00:27:04,200
of life-saving above-ground storm shelters

480
00:27:04,201 --> 00:27:09,500
and reinforced safe rooms built directly into home floor plans.

481
00:27:09,501 --> 00:27:12,900
But a better shelter is only half the answer.

482
00:27:12,901 --> 00:27:17,000
Timely prediction is the key to reducing the carnage.

483
00:27:17,001 --> 00:27:19,500
The National Weather Service monitors storm cells

484
00:27:19,501 --> 00:27:22,660
around the clock and issues alerts when conditions are

485
00:27:22,661 --> 00:27:24,000
favorable for tornadoes.

486
00:27:27,160 --> 00:27:31,040
In Norman, Oklahoma, scientists at the National Severe Storms

487
00:27:31,041 --> 00:27:34,040
and the University of Oklahoma unlock the mysteries

488
00:27:34,140 --> 00:27:37,540
of tornado dynamics with the 800-pound gorilla

489
00:27:37,640 --> 00:27:43,280
of radar systems, the cutting-edge smart mobile Doppler.

490
00:27:43,380 --> 00:27:45,280
It allows us to take this Doppler weather

491
00:27:45,380 --> 00:27:48,200
radar into hurricanes and tornadic storm environments,

492
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:52,200
where we can actually look close and see the very low-level

493
00:27:52,300 --> 00:27:56,200
parts of the storm where the most severe weather is occurring.

494
00:27:56,300 --> 00:27:58,040
This data is later used to help

495
00:27:58,140 --> 00:28:00,300
build tornado computer models.

496
00:28:00,301 --> 00:28:03,100
We don't yet completely understand

497
00:28:03,200 --> 00:28:05,240
how it is that tornadoes form.

498
00:28:05,340 --> 00:28:08,780
We have hypotheses, but they need to be tested.

499
00:28:08,880 --> 00:28:11,120
Using a computer model, we can go ahead

500
00:28:11,220 --> 00:28:13,780
and change the way the winds vary with height

501
00:28:13,880 --> 00:28:15,020
in the atmosphere.

502
00:28:15,120 --> 00:28:17,520
We can change the way temperature and humidity

503
00:28:17,620 --> 00:28:18,920
vary with height.

504
00:28:19,020 --> 00:28:22,360
And we can see how the atmosphere would respond

505
00:28:22,460 --> 00:28:25,560
to these different types of conditions.

506
00:28:25,660 --> 00:28:28,420
The hope is that one day the National Weather Service

507
00:28:28,421 --> 00:28:30,580
can actually predict lethal tornadoes

508
00:28:30,680 --> 00:28:34,260
with even greater lead time, saving countless lives.

509
00:28:38,100 --> 00:28:42,060
A tornado is undoubtedly a killer force of nature.

510
00:28:42,160 --> 00:28:44,940
But statistically, someone in your family

511
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:49,080
is in far greater danger, playing the most dangerous team sport.

512
00:28:55,380 --> 00:28:57,580
It's a statistical fact that many of America's

513
00:28:57,581 --> 00:29:01,820
favorite sports are dangerous.

514
00:29:01,920 --> 00:29:07,620
Some 22,000 people are injured every year playing ice hockey.

515
00:29:07,720 --> 00:29:12,120
Some 120,000 broken bones, torn muscles, and head injuries

516
00:29:12,220 --> 00:29:15,540
happen annually on the baseball diamond.

517
00:29:15,640 --> 00:29:17,540
The Centers for Disease Control estimates

518
00:29:17,640 --> 00:29:22,540
Americans suffer between 1.6 and 3.8 million sports-related

519
00:29:22,640 --> 00:29:26,040
concussions every year.

520
00:29:26,041 --> 00:29:28,500
The statistical leader in concussive events,

521
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:32,440
and therefore the most dangerous, is football.

522
00:29:38,220 --> 00:29:41,120
The physics associated with ever larger, more powerful

523
00:29:41,220 --> 00:29:45,020
athletes applying brutal hits can spell disaster

524
00:29:45,120 --> 00:29:47,220
for the brain.

525
00:29:47,320 --> 00:29:49,760
In professional and college level games,

526
00:29:49,860 --> 00:29:53,140
players are sometimes traveling more than 20 miles per hour

527
00:29:53,240 --> 00:29:54,940
when helmet impacts occur.

528
00:29:54,941 --> 00:30:00,340
You see, this is a brain, and it is surrounded by a vault.

529
00:30:00,440 --> 00:30:01,640
That vault is a skull.

530
00:30:01,740 --> 00:30:05,820
When a concussion occurs, there is movement, movement,

531
00:30:05,920 --> 00:30:07,920
acceleration, then sudden deceleration.

532
00:30:11,260 --> 00:30:13,160
As the brain decelerates,

533
00:30:13,260 --> 00:30:17,060
it impacts against the skull, causing bruising.

534
00:30:17,160 --> 00:30:20,100
In severe cases, the brain can actually bounce back

535
00:30:20,200 --> 00:30:23,700
and strike the other side of the skull.

536
00:30:23,701 --> 00:30:26,700
It's a collision sport, and injuries do happen.

537
00:30:26,800 --> 00:30:29,700
And sometimes what happens is it could be the lightest hit

538
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:31,800
that will turn the neck the wrong way,

539
00:30:31,900 --> 00:30:33,040
and one of them's down on the ground

540
00:30:33,140 --> 00:30:35,340
injured with a concussion.

541
00:30:35,440 --> 00:30:37,240
The majority of dangerous concussions

542
00:30:37,340 --> 00:30:40,660
occur not among pros, but younger athletes

543
00:30:40,760 --> 00:30:42,160
whose brains are still developing.

544
00:30:45,660 --> 00:30:48,560
It is particularly dangerous for individuals

545
00:30:48,660 --> 00:30:53,700
age 18 and younger because of the related growth

546
00:30:53,701 --> 00:30:58,700
and the risk for herniation and edema or swelling that

547
00:30:58,800 --> 00:31:00,700
occurs in young brands.

548
00:31:00,800 --> 00:31:03,340
The first line of defense is the helmet.

549
00:31:05,880 --> 00:31:08,280
21st century sporting goods manufacturers

550
00:31:08,380 --> 00:31:11,080
are racing to build the most advanced protective headgear

551
00:31:11,180 --> 00:31:17,760
in history, a history that began more than a century ago.

552
00:31:17,860 --> 00:31:22,260
In 1893, a lineman for Navy, Joe Reeves,

553
00:31:22,261 --> 00:31:26,560
the first crude football helmet in the Army-Navy game, later

554
00:31:26,660 --> 00:31:30,400
giving birth to the term leatherheads.

555
00:31:30,500 --> 00:31:33,340
The leatherhead gear offered little protection,

556
00:31:33,440 --> 00:31:39,040
and it wasn't until the 1930s that helmets became mandatory.

557
00:31:39,140 --> 00:31:42,440
After World War II, plastics revolutionized the game

558
00:31:42,540 --> 00:31:45,780
with vastly more protective helmets.

559
00:31:45,880 --> 00:31:50,480
Over the past 60 years, Schutz Sports in Salem, Illinois,

560
00:31:50,481 --> 00:31:53,480
has churned out millions of helmets for pro, college,

561
00:31:53,580 --> 00:31:57,120
high school, and even peewee leagues.

562
00:31:57,220 --> 00:31:58,880
Their genesis?

563
00:31:58,980 --> 00:32:02,520
Millions of polycarbonate resin pellets.

564
00:32:02,620 --> 00:32:05,560
When heated to 450 degrees Fahrenheit,

565
00:32:05,660 --> 00:32:10,900
the resin melts and injects into a custom helmet mold.

566
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:14,600
After a quick baking and cooling, a precision robotic drill

567
00:32:14,700 --> 00:32:19,300
creates all of the ventilation and hardware holes.

568
00:32:19,301 --> 00:32:22,840
Lastly, the paint shop mixes and matches thousands of colors

569
00:32:22,940 --> 00:32:25,800
before the helmets get to the gridiron.

570
00:32:25,900 --> 00:32:29,280
Beneath the glossy hues, Schutt's most advanced helmet

571
00:32:29,380 --> 00:32:33,840
is a far cry from the foam-padded relics of the past.

572
00:32:33,940 --> 00:32:37,980
This is the Schutt ION-4D helmet,

573
00:32:38,080 --> 00:32:43,560
and it's our newest helmet in the lineup of helmet protection.

574
00:32:43,660 --> 00:32:47,660
With the helmet, we have the Skydex padding system,

575
00:32:47,661 --> 00:32:50,660
which is a three-dimensional shaped padding system,

576
00:32:50,760 --> 00:32:54,400
which fits much closer to the player's head.

577
00:32:54,500 --> 00:32:57,900
The ION-4D's thermoplastic Skydex padding

578
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:00,900
is designed with opposing outer and inner hemispheres.

579
00:33:04,040 --> 00:33:07,120
At impact, the hemispheres collapse together

580
00:33:07,220 --> 00:33:11,120
and absorb more energy from nasty hits.

581
00:33:11,220 --> 00:33:14,420
Beyond that, the ION's face guard adds more protection

582
00:33:14,520 --> 00:33:17,420
over the standard bolt-on cage.

583
00:33:17,421 --> 00:33:21,020
The face guard slips directly into two ports built into the

584
00:33:21,120 --> 00:33:22,720
helmet.

585
00:33:22,820 --> 00:33:27,460
A thermoplastic rubber energy wedge holds the cage in place.

586
00:33:27,560 --> 00:33:30,960
At impact, the face guard compresses into the wedge,

587
00:33:31,060 --> 00:33:37,280
dampening the force of the hit by as much as 15%.

588
00:33:37,380 --> 00:33:40,580
Engineers at SHUT create new helmet designs and a 3D

589
00:33:40,680 --> 00:33:41,880
modeling pad system.

590
00:33:41,881 --> 00:33:45,580
system.

591
00:33:45,581 --> 00:33:47,960
NOXI, the National Operating Committee

592
00:33:47,961 --> 00:33:50,460
on Standards for Athletic Equipment,

593
00:33:50,461 --> 00:33:52,280
provides strict performance standards

594
00:33:52,281 --> 00:33:55,820
that the helmets must meet.

595
00:33:55,821 --> 00:34:00,460
New design concepts are analyzed in the impact test facility.

596
00:34:00,461 --> 00:34:03,740
In this hall of horrors, replicant human heads

597
00:34:03,741 --> 00:34:07,240
get some major headaches so athletes don't have to.

598
00:34:07,241 --> 00:34:10,980
The NOXI headforms are somewhat quasi-humanoid

599
00:34:10,981 --> 00:34:13,680
to get the harmonics more like the human head.

600
00:34:13,681 --> 00:34:20,100
Inside the head form is a cranial vault, a skull.

601
00:34:20,101 --> 00:34:23,860
This is filled with a glycerin material

602
00:34:23,861 --> 00:34:27,760
very close to the specific gravity of the brain matter.

603
00:34:27,761 --> 00:34:30,660
And embedded in the center of gravity of the head form

604
00:34:30,661 --> 00:34:35,980
is the triaxial accelerometer that measures the g-forces.

605
00:34:35,981 --> 00:34:38,880
The standard NOXI drop test simulates a player

606
00:34:38,881 --> 00:34:44,360
impacting an immovable object at nearly 13 miles per hour,

607
00:34:44,361 --> 00:34:46,780
about like running head first into a brick wall.

608
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:52,820
The computer records the severity index,

609
00:34:52,821 --> 00:34:55,520
or the number of g-forces the humanoid head form will

610
00:34:55,521 --> 00:34:59,160
experience at impact.

611
00:34:59,161 --> 00:35:03,000
This head form experienced 108 g's.

612
00:35:03,001 --> 00:35:06,400
Compare that to the same test inflicted on a vintage 1960s

613
00:35:06,401 --> 00:35:08,800
padded with old school suspension webbing.

614
00:35:20,140 --> 00:35:24,860
This head form experienced a whopping 218 Gs,

615
00:35:24,861 --> 00:35:29,360
more than double the trauma compared to the shut helmet.

616
00:35:29,361 --> 00:35:31,300
But the vast majority of helmet hits

617
00:35:31,301 --> 00:35:34,820
are not of the brick wall variety.

618
00:35:34,821 --> 00:35:37,520
Most will impact the brain from different angles and speeds.

619
00:35:40,460 --> 00:35:43,400
To simulate these hits, the linear impact test

620
00:35:43,401 --> 00:35:44,760
propels a 30-pound ram.

621
00:35:48,840 --> 00:35:52,680
In this test, it's somewhat more real world.

622
00:35:52,681 --> 00:35:59,480
It has a three-dummy neck that's fairly rigid, but flexible.

623
00:35:59,481 --> 00:36:04,020
It also has a roller bearing table

624
00:36:04,021 --> 00:36:09,080
to where when it's struck that the body and the head form

625
00:36:09,081 --> 00:36:12,060
move with the impact.

626
00:36:12,061 --> 00:36:17,140
A final integrity check serves up a helmet on ice.

627
00:36:17,141 --> 00:36:19,800
The cold crack test requires freezing a helmet

628
00:36:19,801 --> 00:36:23,780
to zero degrees Fahrenheit to make it brittle

629
00:36:23,781 --> 00:36:26,840
and then hitting it with a 20-pound projectile dropped

630
00:36:26,841 --> 00:36:29,240
through a tube.

631
00:36:29,340 --> 00:36:31,840
If it doesn't crack now, it likely never will.

632
00:36:35,680 --> 00:36:38,460
We have many materials that we have an ongoing research

633
00:36:38,560 --> 00:36:41,120
of looking at better shock attenuation materials,

634
00:36:41,220 --> 00:36:43,920
better plastics that would be able to be used,

635
00:36:44,020 --> 00:36:46,560
looking for the best combination of things.

636
00:36:49,700 --> 00:36:51,360
No one disputes that a brain injury

637
00:36:51,460 --> 00:36:54,260
is a serious event.

638
00:36:54,360 --> 00:36:56,300
But when you leap off a bridge over the snake

639
00:36:56,301 --> 00:36:57,200
Canyon.

640
00:36:57,300 --> 00:36:58,300
Oh.

641
00:36:58,400 --> 00:37:01,100
A concussion doesn't compare to what could happen if you make

642
00:37:01,200 --> 00:37:02,300
the slightest mistake.

643
00:37:08,040 --> 00:37:10,740
It's a crystal clear day in Twin Falls, Idaho.

644
00:37:13,560 --> 00:37:15,060
Perfect conditions for a jump.

645
00:37:18,560 --> 00:37:21,360
Chris Wilson is a veteran skydiver.

646
00:37:21,460 --> 00:37:24,800
But today, there is no airplane.

647
00:37:24,801 --> 00:37:28,400
Today, he'll leap from the I.B. Perrine Bridge,

648
00:37:28,500 --> 00:37:33,280
485 feet above the Snake River Canyon.

649
00:37:33,380 --> 00:37:35,920
It's his first attempt at an extreme sport

650
00:37:36,020 --> 00:37:37,780
that many consider the most dangerous.

651
00:37:40,920 --> 00:37:42,520
I think when I climb over that railing,

652
00:37:42,620 --> 00:37:44,020
there might be a little bit of nervousness.

653
00:37:44,120 --> 00:37:47,520
But as of now, I feel pretty confident and pretty good.

654
00:37:47,620 --> 00:37:51,820
Base jumping bears little resemblance to skydiving.

655
00:37:51,920 --> 00:37:54,760
Leaping off fixed positions so close to the sky

656
00:37:54,800 --> 00:37:58,400
presents a multitude of hazards.

657
00:37:58,500 --> 00:38:01,800
Unpredictable wind gusts, sheer rock bases,

658
00:38:01,900 --> 00:38:05,440
speeding by just feet away, and the precious few seconds

659
00:38:05,540 --> 00:38:11,040
to pull the chute mean any given jump could be your last.

660
00:38:11,140 --> 00:38:16,760
BASE is an acronym for Building Antenna Span Earth.

661
00:38:16,860 --> 00:38:19,600
To qualify for a recognized BASE membership,

662
00:38:19,700 --> 00:38:23,800
a person must successfully jump all four.

663
00:38:23,801 --> 00:38:27,040
There are an estimated 4,000 recognized base jumpers

664
00:38:27,140 --> 00:38:32,440
worldwide, with approximately 120 estimated deaths to date.

665
00:38:32,540 --> 00:38:34,840
The accident ratio in this sport is extreme.

666
00:38:38,940 --> 00:38:43,460
One in 33 jumpers will die.

667
00:38:43,560 --> 00:38:46,460
Unlike other jump locations, the Parine Bridge

668
00:38:46,560 --> 00:38:49,460
is the only spot in the United States where base jumpers don't

669
00:38:49,560 --> 00:38:51,160
have to fear legal prosecution.

670
00:38:51,161 --> 00:38:57,340
prosecution, but not without a courtesy call to the Twin Falls

671
00:38:57,341 --> 00:38:58,340
police.

672
00:38:58,341 --> 00:39:00,640
Yeah, we just have to call them because there's

673
00:39:00,641 --> 00:39:02,780
been a couple of times when somebody driving across the

674
00:39:02,781 --> 00:39:04,640
bridge has seen someone jump, called in thinking they saw

675
00:39:04,641 --> 00:39:08,780
suicide, and then they have to send out a car and see what's

676
00:39:08,781 --> 00:39:10,380
happening.

677
00:39:10,381 --> 00:39:14,120
Tom Aiello runs the Snake River Base Academy.

678
00:39:14,121 --> 00:39:19,020
He calls his four-day first-timers class Death Camp.

679
00:39:19,021 --> 00:39:21,480
Day one orientation requires each student

680
00:39:21,481 --> 00:39:25,640
to write a letter to relatives, explaining why he or she died

681
00:39:25,641 --> 00:39:26,380
face jumping.

682
00:39:26,880 --> 00:39:29,580
It makes the students sit and think about the reality

683
00:39:29,581 --> 00:39:30,281
of what they're doing.

684
00:39:30,400 --> 00:39:31,700
We're all connected to other people,

685
00:39:32,220 --> 00:39:35,480
and they're connected to their families and their friends,

686
00:39:35,481 --> 00:39:37,720
and they need to think about the impact on those people

687
00:39:37,721 --> 00:39:38,860
of the decisions they make.

688
00:39:40,760 --> 00:39:43,320
The American flag marks the landing zone.

689
00:39:44,500 --> 00:39:47,800
Land anywhere else, and the danger magnifies even more.

690
00:39:49,020 --> 00:39:53,760
See where the wooden footbridge is over that little gully?

691
00:39:53,860 --> 00:39:56,660
From there to the bridge is bad landing, OK?

692
00:39:56,760 --> 00:39:59,760
The gully itself is about six or eight feet deep.

693
00:39:59,860 --> 00:40:01,760
And on this side, there's a bunch of boulders

694
00:40:01,860 --> 00:40:03,460
hiding in those weeds, OK?

695
00:40:03,560 --> 00:40:06,680
So you don't want to try and land there.

696
00:40:06,780 --> 00:40:10,180
An early rehearsal helps to ease the tension.

697
00:40:10,280 --> 00:40:11,380
This is it, man.

698
00:40:11,480 --> 00:40:14,520
This is the moment I've been waiting for.

699
00:40:14,620 --> 00:40:17,020
But every jumper knows there is one next factor

700
00:40:17,021 --> 00:40:23,260
that could spell disaster, a parachute malfunction.

701
00:40:23,360 --> 00:40:27,900
The Academy spends hours packing and repacking chutes.

702
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:31,700
Unlike skydivers who carry a main and a reserve chute,

703
00:40:31,800 --> 00:40:34,400
base jumpers don't have the luxury of time and free fall

704
00:40:34,500 --> 00:40:36,240
to determine if their chute is faulty.

705
00:40:38,900 --> 00:40:42,680
For them, there is no time to deploy a reserve chute.

706
00:40:42,780 --> 00:40:46,820
They rely on one and only one parachute that's similar in size

707
00:40:46,821 --> 00:40:48,420
to a skydiving reserve canopy.

708
00:40:51,320 --> 00:40:53,460
This reality has helped darken the history

709
00:40:53,560 --> 00:40:58,560
of even a user-friendly jump site, like the Perrine Bridge.

710
00:40:58,660 --> 00:41:01,460
Six people have met their death, and several more

711
00:41:01,560 --> 00:41:02,860
have been injured jumping here.

712
00:41:05,980 --> 00:41:08,980
Most of the tragedies occurred when shoots failed to deploy.

713
00:41:14,180 --> 00:41:16,380
To prevent this, base jumpers typically

714
00:41:16,381 --> 00:41:19,020
a small pilot shoot that causes the main shoot

715
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:20,020
to deploy effectively.

716
00:41:25,020 --> 00:41:25,820
Oh.

717
00:41:25,920 --> 00:41:27,420
You want to talk about that?

718
00:41:27,520 --> 00:41:28,520
Oh, man.

719
00:41:28,620 --> 00:41:30,620
Yeah, he fumbled the pilot shoot.

720
00:41:30,720 --> 00:41:33,140
And then, because he had fumbled, he was kicking

721
00:41:33,240 --> 00:41:34,840
and threw himself in a line twist.

722
00:41:34,940 --> 00:41:36,740
The line twist happened because his body position

723
00:41:36,840 --> 00:41:38,040
got thrown off by missing.

724
00:41:38,140 --> 00:41:39,140
All right, let's do it.

725
00:41:39,240 --> 00:41:40,340
All right.

726
00:41:40,440 --> 00:41:41,640
To ensure this won't happen to him,

727
00:41:41,740 --> 00:41:45,640
Chris will perform what's known as a PCA, or pilot shoot

728
00:41:45,641 --> 00:41:48,400
to pilot shoot assist.

729
00:41:48,401 --> 00:41:49,280
I'm ready.

730
00:41:49,281 --> 00:41:51,280
Tom will hold on to the pilot shoot

731
00:41:51,281 --> 00:41:54,280
and help deploy the main parachute as Chris falls.

732
00:41:54,281 --> 00:41:55,180
Ready?

733
00:41:55,181 --> 00:41:59,080
3, 2, 1, I'm out.

734
00:41:59,081 --> 00:42:00,280
Ah!

735
00:42:00,281 --> 00:42:01,380
Yeah!

736
00:42:01,381 --> 00:42:02,300
Whoo!

737
00:42:07,200 --> 00:42:09,200
Chris's jump looked really good.

738
00:42:09,201 --> 00:42:10,540
Aside from at the very end, I think

739
00:42:10,541 --> 00:42:12,000
he should have turned around a little sooner.

740
00:42:12,001 --> 00:42:14,400
So it was good.

741
00:42:14,401 --> 00:42:15,400
How'd that jump go?

742
00:42:15,401 --> 00:42:16,300
It's amazing.

743
00:42:16,301 --> 00:42:17,400
Stand up landing.

744
00:42:17,401 --> 00:42:18,300
I'm all in one piece.

745
00:42:18,301 --> 00:42:20,800
So rock on.

746
00:42:20,801 --> 00:42:23,640
Another day sets on death count.

747
00:42:23,641 --> 00:42:26,440
The hope is that it will never live up to its name.

748
00:42:26,441 --> 00:42:27,780
The margin of error is so small.

749
00:42:27,781 --> 00:42:30,640
You really don't have time to fix any mistakes.

750
00:42:30,641 --> 00:42:33,660
You know, something happens and you don't react quick enough.

751
00:42:33,661 --> 00:42:35,320
See ya.

752
00:42:35,321 --> 00:42:36,780
Base jumping is dangerous.

753
00:42:36,781 --> 00:42:39,920
There's nothing you can do to make it completely safe.

754
00:42:39,921 --> 00:42:42,860
And quite honestly, that's part of the appeal for some people.

755
00:42:42,861 --> 00:42:46,600
It's facing a danger and overcoming it.

756
00:42:46,700 --> 00:42:48,700
Those who encounter the most dangerous

757
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:50,760
come in all types.

758
00:42:50,860 --> 00:42:54,740
Some embrace the danger with a relentless passion.

759
00:42:54,840 --> 00:42:58,500
Others weigh the risks but still play the game,

760
00:42:58,600 --> 00:43:01,180
while others wait in the shadows and silently pray

761
00:43:01,280 --> 00:43:02,340
they are spared.

762
00:43:02,440 --> 00:43:04,840
Watch behind us, guys.

763
00:43:04,940 --> 00:43:07,920
None of us is immune to the danger.

764
00:43:08,020 --> 00:43:12,860
Our only say in the matter is how well we cope with it.

765
00:43:12,861 --> 00:43:14,920
you

